{
  "image_name": "60343f6d7c285105373c099867884698.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares the same solid oxide cell operated in two opposite modes. In both cases, a dense electrolyte sits between a fuel electrode on top and an oxygen electrode below, and oxide ions move through the electrolyte while gases react at the porous electrodes.\n\nOn the left, the device works as an SOEC, or electrolysis cell. Electrical power is supplied, water enters the fuel electrode, and it is reduced to hydrogen while generating oxide ions. Those oxide ions travel through the electrolyte to the oxygen side, where they are oxidized to oxygen and release electrons. The overall effect is conversion of water into hydrogen and oxygen using input electricity.\n\nOn the right, the process is reversed in SOFC mode, functioning as a fuel cell. Hydrogen enters the fuel electrode and reacts with oxide ions to form water and electrons. At the oxygen electrode, oxygen gains electrons to form oxide ions, which then migrate through the electrolyte toward the fuel side. This direction produces electrical power as output.\n\nOverall, the image highlights the reversible chemistry and opposite ion/electron flow in electrolysis versus fuel-cell operation.",
